# Files to be ignored by git in a DataFlex workspace

# AppHtml
**/apphtml/global.asa

# AppSrc
**/appsrc/**/*.prn
**/appsrc/**/*.dep
**/appsrc/**/*.dbg
**/appsrc/**/*.exe
**/appsrc/**/*.exe.manifest
# ...except the fixed manifests a df-cli build needs (it never writes one): UnitTests and the DUF updater.
!/AppSrc/UnitTests64.exe.manifest
!/AppSrc/DUFUpdateProgram64.exe.manifest
# ...except the test program's, a fixed file the build embeds (DFUnit)
!**/appsrc/**/UnitTests*.exe.manifest
**/appsrc/**/*.err
**/appsrc/**/*.fld
**/appsrc/**/*.pbg
**/appsrc/**/*.pdp
**/appsrc/**/*.pkd
**/appsrc/**/*.prn
**/appsrc/**/*.prp
**/appsrc/config/classlist.xml
loginEncryptionkey.inc
# ...except THIS workspace's: a public key, committed on purpose - DFRefactor stores no passwords
# (see the file itself). Every other workspace's key stays out of git.
!/AppSrc/LoginEncryptionKey.inc

# Data
# *.log* rather than *.log, because this tool names its run logs "<Program>.log.txt" and that
# extension slips straight past a plain *.log. HarvestWin32Projection.log.txt sat in git status
# as permanent noise until 2026-08-30; MetaTagCheck had already hit the same naming and got a
# bespoke line for it further down (it writes to the repo root, not here). Widening beats a
# second one-off, since the next maintainer tool will be named the same way. Every run rewrites
# these, so none is worth a version. Safe: no tracked file under Data\ carries ".log" anywhere
# in its name - statlog.dat and friends put the dot after "log", not before it.
**/Data/**/*.log*
**/Data/**/*.dat
**/Data/**/*.vld
**/Data/**/*.k*
**/Data/**/*.bad
**/Data/**/*.hdr
**/Data/**/*.cch
**/Data/**/*.cfg
**/Data/**/*.tag
**/Data/**/Deploy/**
**/Data/compileheader.h
**/Data/ScintillaEdit.ini
**/Data/DUFUpdateLog.txt
# Per-workspace saved refactoring plan (last analyzer triage, frozen for "View last plan"). The
# filename folds the .sws path, so it's machine-specific + regenerated on every analyze - never share.
**/Data/LastPlan*.json
# Per-workspace analyzer Ignore list (dismissed report-only rows). Same deal: folds the .sws path,
# machine-specific, rebuilt as the user ignores rows - never share.
**/Data/Ignored*.json
# Per-workspace modernization progress + history. Folds the .sws path like the two above, so the
# FILENAME alone names the workspace it was run against - and those are customer workspaces. Never
# let these reach a repo; origin is public.
**/Data/ModProgress*.json
# Per-workspace Deep Scan cache (last-write + size + hits per file), so a program restart does not
# pay a cold scan. Same folded-.sws filename as the three above, so the same rule applies - and it
# additionally CONTAINS customer source paths, one per scanned file.
**/Data/DeepScanCache*.json
# RETIRED 2026-09-15: the per-workspace record of which shadowing copies [Rename them] had
# retired. It existed to leave a copy the user renamed BACK alone for ever after, and that
# exception is gone - nothing reads or writes these files any more. The rule stays so the ones
# already on disk keep out of the way; it folds the .sws path, so the filename alone names a
# workspace, and the contents are that workspace's source paths.
**/Data/RetiredShadows*.json
# Per-workspace record of the overriding copies the user DECIDED to keep - written only by the
# Rename dialog's "keep the other M" (2026-09-15), never inferred. Folds the .sws path like the
# rules above, so the filename alone names a workspace, and it CONTAINS that workspace's source paths.
**/Data/KeptShadows*.json
# Per-workspace record of the files a refactoring plan run left unchanged, with its functions (cPlanNoOps,
# 2026-11-12) - machine-local run state, like LastPlan.
**/Data/PlanNoOps*.json
# Test fixtures the DFUnit suite writes into Data\ (the kept-shadows round-trip file). Regenerated per run.
# The scratch tWinStructs.pkg fixtures of the shipped-struct tests go to the Windows temp folder since
# 2026-10-05; their rule stays for a test runner built before that, which still writes them here.
**/Data/_tWinStructs*.pkg
**/Data/_KeptShadowsTest.json
# Saved editor settings: colours, hotkeys, the lot. Personal, and rewritten on every settings change.
# (The .ini form beside it is already ignored - this is the JSON that replaced it.)
**/Data/ScintillaEdit.json
# Deep Scan rule toggles. Seeded from BuildDefaultDeepScanRules and then owned by the user's
# Enabled checkboxes - the SEED is the tracked thing, not one machine's answer to it.
**/Data/DeepScanRules.json
# LEGACY NAMES of the catalogues below, from before the DfVersion- prefix (2026-08-01). Nothing
# writes or reads these any more; they are leftovers on machines that ran an earlier build. Ignored
# so they stay out of the way rather than deleted - they are the user's files, and harmless.
**/Data/TypeDefs-*.json
**/Data/ObsoleteScan-*.json
**/Data/GlobalFuncNames-*.json
**/Data/GlobalBuiltIns-*.json
**/Data/DfLanguage-*.json

# THE PER-VERSION CATALOGUES - TRACKED ON PURPOSE, all six of them.
#   DfVersion-Language-<ver>.json       keywords, operators, scope PAIRS, meta tags   (Lib\DataFlex.lng)
#   DfVersion-BuiltIns-<ver>.json       the compiler's built-in globals               (Lib\GlobalFunctions.lst)
#   DfVersion-TypeDefs-<ver>.json       Type/struct definitions                       (Pkg walk)
#   DfVersion-ObsoleteScan-<ver>.json   the install's obsolete-warning recipes        (Pkg walk)
#   DfVersion-GlobalFuncs-<ver>.json    Global function names declared in Pkg source  (Pkg walk)
#   DfVersion-ErrorNames-<ver>.json     the named error numbers (DFERR_*)             (Pkg\errornum.inc)
#
# One shared prefix, one rule, and a NEW catalogue is covered the day it is added - which four
# separate exceptions could not promise. The prefix says the true thing about all of them: each is a
# fact about a DataFlex VERSION, read from that version's own install, and none of them changes
# unless the install does. (Naming them all "Language" would have made the glob work and the names
# lie - TypeDefs are structs, ObsoleteScan are recipes.)
#
# Committing them buys two things: `git diff` answers "what did this release change?" - which is the
# DF 27 question - and a fresh clone starts with warm caches instead of paying the cold Pkg walk.
# They cannot churn meaningfully: identical installs write identical files, and a machine whose
# install genuinely differs produces a diff worth seeing.
#
# Written for a CHOSEN version by the TestBench "Write catalogues..." tool; written for the CURRENT
# target by the product itself, at the same paths.
!**/Data/DfVersion-*.json

# Scratch left by an interrupted or instrumented run.
**/Data/PlanPhases.log.txt
**/Data/_zv*

# DDSrc
**/ddsrc/**/*.def

